.Dd August 15, 2026
.Dt CTRLAPP 3
.Os libcontrol
.Sh NAME
.Nm ctrlapp ,
.Nm ctrlapp_set ,
.Nm ctrlapp_getname ,
.Nm ctrlapp_getclass ,
.Nm ctrlapp_getargc ,
.Nm ctrlapp_getargv
.Nd utility functions for application identification and initialization
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In X11/Xlib.h
.In control/application.h
.Ft void
.Fo ctrlapp_set
.Fa "char const *name"
.Fa "char const *class"
.Fa "int argc"
.Fa "char **argv"
.Fc
.Ft "char const *"
.Fo "ctrlapp_getname"
.Fc
.Ft "char const *"
.Fo "ctrlapp_getclass"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo "ctrlapp_getargc"
.Fc
.Ft "char **"
.Fo "ctrlapp_getargv"
.Fc
.Ft "Display *"
.Fo "ctrlapp_open"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo ctrlapp_visuals
.Fa "Display *display"
.Fa "int screen_number"
.Fa "int *depth_ret"
.Fa "Colormap *colormap_ret"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The ctrlapp library implements utility routines to identify the current application,
and initialize it:
.Bl -bullet -compact -width ""
.It
Some setters and getters of global properties for the current application.
Similar to
.Xr setprogname 3
and
.Xr getprogname 3 .
.It
A wrapper routine around
.Xr XOpenDisplay 3
to handle openning a connection to the X server.
.It
A wrapper routine around
.Xr XMatchVisualInfo 3
to get 32bit-deep true-color visuals.
.El
.Ss Application properties
The
.Fn ctrlapp_set
function sets the name, class, and command-line arguments of the current program.
The storage for each array argument must remain valid for the rest of the program's lifetime.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlapp_getname ,
.Fn ctrlapp_getclass ,
.Fn ctrlapp_getargc ,
and
.Fn ctrlapp_getargv
functions return the name, class, and command-line arguments of the current program,
as previously set by
.Fn ctrlapp_set .
In fact, the same addresses are returned, for they are all saved in a static global variable.
.Pp
These routines are used by other ctrl* libraries to avoid having to pass to every function
such strings that are constant through the entire program's lifetime.
.Pp
The parameters are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "XXXXXXXXXX"
.It Ar name
The application's name, for
.Xr XClassHint 3 .
.It Ar class
The application's class, for
.Xr XClassHint 3 .
.It Ar argc
The number of command-line arguments, as given to
.Fn main .
.It Ar argv
The array of command-line arguments, as given to
.Fn main .
.El
.Pp
If the value of the
.Ar name
or
.Ar class
argument is
.Dv NULL
(or if
.Fn ctrlapp_set
has not been called),
then
.Fn ctrlapp_getname
and
.Fn ctrlapp_getclass
return the same as
.Xr getprogname 3 .
.Ss Connecting to X server
The
.Fn ctrlapp_open
routine is a wrapper around
.Xr XOpenDisplay 3
that, in addition to open a connection to X server, does the following:
.Pp
It sets the process'
.Xr locale 1
(some font routines need it to display unicode characters correctly).
If the locale could not be set, or the server does not support it
(see
.Xr XSupportsLocale 3 ) ,
.Fn ctrlapp_open
writes a warning message on the standard error.
.Pp
It reads a display name from the
.Ev DISPLAY
environment variable,
and opens a connection to the X server identified by such name.
It is an error if this environment variable is not set, and
.Fn ctrlapp_open
returns
.Dv NULL and
writes a warning message into the standard error.
.Pp
It sets the close-on-exec
.Pq Dv FD_CLOEXEC
flag on the display's file descriptor,
so it is closed if the application ever call a function from the
.Xr execve 2
family.
.Ss 32-bit TrueColor visuals
The
.Fn ctrlapp_visuals
routine is a wrapper around
.Xr XMatchVisualInfo 3
that finds and returns (on the arguments passed as reference)
a 32bit-deep, true-color visual supported by the given
.Ar display
and
.Ar screen_number .
.Pp
The parameters are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "XXXXXXXXXXXXX"
.It Ar display
The pointer to the context of the connection to the X server.
.It Ar screen_number
The index to the graphics card that the X server manages
(there's only one for most modern computers,
so its value is often zero)
.It Ar visual_ret
The address where to store the 32bit-deep true-color visual at.
If such visual is not supported, store the display's default visual instead.
.It Ar colormap_ret
The address where to store the colormap for the 32bit-deep true-color visual at.
If such visual is not supported, store the display's default colormap instead.
.It Ar depth_ret
The address where to store the number
.Dv 32
at.
If 32bit-deep colors is not supported,
store the display's default depth instead.
.El
.Pp
It returns non-zero if the 32bit-deep true-color visual is supported,
and stores its visual/colormap/depth at the arguments passed as reference.
If ucht visual is not supported,
it returns zero and stores the display's default visual/colormap/depth instead.
